 ],
 "literal": "Because a festival has commenced, because his wife has given birth / on a day when rain has fallen and the sun is setting / the hand of the leather-worker who weaves a cot / was swift, like the needle pushing the leather strip, indeed! / With the warrior who came to capture the town / the battle of the great noble hero wearing an Ātti garland [is as swift].",
 "translation": "A festival has begun; his wife has just given birth; and the rain-laden sun is setting. At this urgent hour, the hand of the leather-worker weaving a cot moves with incredible speed, pushing the needle through the leather strips. Even swifter than that needle is the battle waged by our great noble king, adorned with a garland of Ātti flowers, against the enemy warrior who came to capture his town!",
 "modern": "விழா தொடங்கிவிட்டது; அவனுடைய மனைவி குழந்தை பெற்றெடுத்துக் கிடக்கிறாள்; மழையோடு சூரியனும் மறையும் மாலை நேரம் வந்துவிட்டது. இந்த அவசரமான சூழலில், கட்டில் பின்னும் தொழிலாளியின் கையில் இருக்கும், வாரைச் செலுத்தும் ஊசி எவ்வளவு வேகமாக ஓடுமோ, அதைவிட வேகமாக இருக்கிறது, தன் ஊரைக் கைப்பற்ற வந்த பகை வீரனுடன் போரிடும் ஆத்தி மாலை அணிந்த சோழன் பெருநற்கிள்ளியின் போர் வேகம்!",
 "explanation": "The poet Sāttantaiyār sings in praise of the Chola king Pōrvaikkōp Perunaṟkiḷḷi. The king is engaged in a fierce battle against an invading warrior who has come to capture his town. To describe the incredible speed and urgency of the king's combat, the poet uses a vivid domestic simile of a leather-worker. This craftsman has three pressing reasons to work quickly: a festival has begun, his wife has just given birth, and the sun is setting after a rainy day. His hand moves the needle through the leather strips with lightning speed, yet the king's battle is even swifter. This poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai, celebrating the swift and decisive victory of the king.",
